    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Fault segmentation along dip direction occurs either towards the hanging wall (THW) or the footwall (TFW). We document small-scale field examples for both types and point out the different deformation resulting from each configuration. Hanging wall segmentation is associated with incompetent units that are conformable with the segment, i.e., parallel to it. Footwall segmentation is associated with incompetent units at high angle to the segment and with shortening perpendicular to bedding. Segmentation occurs near competent-incompetent bedding interfaces forcing faults arrest and step laterally. The width of a step between two faults segments increases with the thickness of the incompetent layer. We propose that the two stepping styles form depending on the initial arrangement of the incipient fault segments which form initially in the competent layers. The style of stepping can influence the overall geometry of the fault hen these segments link. In general, linked segments of THW style will form steeper faults than those segments of the TFW. A model is presented to estimate the possible change in fault angle due to the stepping induced by incompetent layers.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
